#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2a6f096-41d9-5250-bd7e-b95d0b6e4a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335383} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3db9ab-4bdc-52c1-bd78-8d2f25ce180e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335384} {:answer/id #uuid "00243150-770f-5f4f-8449-bfc02dcdfce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-2-x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335385} {:answer/id #uuid "feb991f1-4050-58ab-86f0-3cb5c2eb15c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-2+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335386} {:answer/id #uuid "daca5d82-050b-5f20-8c5f-4bab365f7108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daca5d82-050b-5f20-8c5f-4bab365f7108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335387} :answer-field/id #uuid "76acc3b3-2256-500e-853d-ac36a9df1535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02cd9397-a795-5b68-a5d4-f55d0d92f60a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98553" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={e}^{y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x{e}^{y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-1{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇\\,f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+2(x+1)-2(y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
